L000713	\section{Derivation of a class of incompatibility witnesses}\label{appendix: HO ineq}
L000714	Hereafter we derive the incompatibility witness.
L000715	Given that the assemblage $\boldsymbol{O}$ admits the HO model, we obtain
L000716	\begin{equation}
L000717	\begin{aligned}
L000718	    g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{O}_{x})
L000719	    &= \sum_{a} p(a|x) g \left[ \sum_{\lambda}p(\lambda|a,x)O_{\lambda} \right] \\
L000720	    &= \sum_{a} p(a|x) g \left[ \sum_{\lambda}\frac{p(a|x,\lambda)p(\lambda)}{p(a|x)}O_{\lambda} \right] \\
L000721	    &\leq \sum_{a} p(a|x) \sum_{\lambda}\frac{p(a|x,\lambda)p(\lambda)}{p(a|x)} g(O_{\lambda}) \\
L000722	    &= \sum_{\lambda} p(\lambda) g(O_{\lambda}) \\
L000723	    &\leq \sum_{\lambda} p(\lambda) F_{g}(O_{\lambda}),
L000724	\end{aligned}
L000725	\end{equation}
L000726	where the first inequality follows from the convexity of $g$ and the second from the definition $F_{g}\ge g$.
L000727	Because $F_{g}$ is concave in $\mathcal{O}$, we also have
L000728	\begin{equation}
L000729	    \sum_{\lambda} p(\lambda)\,F_{g}(O_{\lambda})
L000730	    \le F_{g}^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{O}_{x}) \quad \forall x.
L000731	\end{equation}
L000732	Combining the two chains of inequalities yields, for every assemblage $\boldsymbol{O}$ that admits the HO model,
L000733	\begin{equation}
L000734	    g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{O}_{x}) \stackrel{\text{\tiny{HO}}}{\leq} F_{g}^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{O}_{x'}) \quad \forall x,x'.
L000735	\end{equation}
L000736	Hence the pair $(g^{\rm as},F_{g}^{\rm as})$ constitutes a valid compatibility witness.
L000737	Analogously, starting from a concave functional $f$ and its (least) convex roof $G_{f}\ge f$, one obtains another class of incompatibility witness
L000738	\begin{equation}
L000739	    f^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{O}_{x})
L000740	    \stackrel{\text{\tiny{HO}}}{\ge}
L000741	    G_{f}^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{O}_{x'}) \quad \forall\,x,x'.
L000742	\end{equation}
L000743	Below we focus on the convex-$g$ version; the concave-$f$ case can be derived analogously.
L000744	As shown in the main text, the incompatibility witness can be defined by
L000745	\begin{equation}
L000746	    \mathcal{V}_{g}(\boldsymbol{O}) := \max \{ \max_{x} g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{O}_{x}) - \min_{x} F_{g}^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{O}_{x}), 0 \}.
L000747	\end{equation}
L000748	
L000749	
L000750	
L000751	For completeness, in the following, we detail the process of constructing a witness in a quantum incompatibility task and demonstrate that the maximizations and minimizations in the method are computationally efficient.
L000752	
L000753	
L000754	Consider a quantum device with $m$ classical inputs (labeled $x \in \{1, 2, \dots, m\}$) and $n$ classical outputs (labeled $a \in \{1, 2, \dots, n\}$).
L000755	For each chosen input $x$, the device stochastically produces an output $a$ and a conditional quantum state $\rho_{a|x}$.
L000756	This experimental setup provides a state assemblage $\boldsymbol{\sigma} = \{ \sigma_{a|x} = p(a|x) \rho_{a|x} \}_{a,x}$, where all $x$ settings share the same marginal state, i.e.,
L000757	\begin{equation}
L000758	    \sum_{a}\sigma_{a|x}=\sum_{a'}\sigma_{a'|x'} \quad \forall\, x,x' \in \{1,2,\dots,m\},
L000759	\end{equation}
L000760	noting that if the marginal states differ, the assemblage is trivially incompatible.
L000761	
L000762	
L000763	For each setting $x$, one calculates the assemblage versions of the functionals:
L000764	$g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_x)$ and $F_g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_x)$.
L000765	The violation degree of the incompatibility witness $\mathcal{S}_g(\boldsymbol{\sigma})$ is then simply given by:
L000766	\begin{equation}
L000767	\begin{aligned}
L000768	    \mathcal{S}_g(\boldsymbol{\sigma})
L000769	    &:=\max\left\{ \max_x g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{x}) - \min_{x'}F_g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{x'}) \, ,0\right\}  \\
L000770	    &=\max \left\{ \max \left\{g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{1}),\dots, g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{m}) \right\}-\min\left\{ F_g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{1}),\dots,F_g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{m})  \right\},\,0\right\}.
L000771	\end{aligned}
L000772	\end{equation}
L000773	As shown above, the required maximization and minimization involve only simple comparisons among $m$ real numbers.
L000774	The computational complexity scales linearly with the number of measurement settings $m$, and the associated computational tasks are negligible compared to existing optimization-based certification techniques~\cite{Cavalcanti_2016}.
L000775	
L000776	
