L000896	\section{Proof of Result 3: The Witnesses Lower bounds a full entanglement measure}\label{appendix: entanglement measure}
L000897	
L000898	Here, we consider the case that Alice and Bob share a pure entangled state $\rho^{\rm AB}$.
L000899	We first show that to obtain the maximal violation degree, the optimal measurements Alice performed are set at most rank one, i.e., ${\rm rank}(M_{a|x})=1~\forall a,x$.
L000900	Let's recall that $\sigma_{a|x}=\Tr_{\rm A}[(M_{a|x}\otimes \id)\, \rho^{\rm AB}]$:
L000901	\begin{equation}
L000902	\begin{aligned}
L000903	    g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{x})
L000904	    &:= \sum_{a}p(a|x)g(\rho_{a|x}) \\
L000905	    &= \sum_{a} p(a|x) g\left[ \frac{{\rm Tr}_{A}(M_{a|x}\ts \id) \rho^{\rm AB}}{p(a|x)} \right],
L000906	\end{aligned}
L000907	\end{equation}
L000908	where $p(a|x)={\rm Tr}\left[(M_{a|x}\ts \id) \rho^{\rm AB} \right]$.
L000909	Here, we can decompose each measurement $M_{a|x}$ into fine-grained measurement $M_{i,a|x}\ge 0$ and ${\rm rng}(M_{i,a|x})\le 1~\forall i,a,x$ such that $\sum_{i} M_{i,a|x} = M_{a|x}~\forall a,x$.
L000910	Under this assumption, we have a fine-grained version of $g^{\rm as}$, that is,
L000911	\begin{equation}
L000912	\begin{aligned}
L000913	    g_{\rm fine}^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{x})
L000914	    &=\sum_{i,a} p(i,a|x) g\left[ \frac{{\rm Tr}_{A}(M_{i,a|x}\ts \id) \rho^{\rm AB}}{p(i,a|x)} \right] \\
L000915	    &= \sum_{a} p(a|x) \sum_{i}\frac{p(i,a|x)}{p(a|x)}g\left[ \frac{{\rm Tr}_{A}(M_{i,a|x}\ts \id) \rho^{\rm AB}}{p(i,a|x)} \right] \\
L000916	    &\geq \sum_{a} p(a|x) g\left[\sum_{i}\frac{p(i,a|x)}{p(a|x)} \frac{{\rm Tr}_{A}(M_{i,a|x}\ts \id) \rho^{\rm AB}}{p(i,a|x)} \right] \\
L000917	    &= \sum_{a} p(a|x) g\left[ \frac{\sum_{i}{\rm Tr}_{A}(M_{i,a|x}\ts \id) \rho^{\rm AB}}{p(a|x)} \right] \\
L000918	    &= g^{\rm as}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{x}),
L000919	\end{aligned}
L000920	\end{equation}
L000921	where $p(i,a|x)={\rm Tr}\left[(M_{i,a|x}\ts \id) \rho^{\rm AB} \right]$.
L000922	The above inequality is obtained by the convexity; therefore, one can readily obtain $F^{\rm as}_{g,\textrm{fine}}\le F^{\rm as}_{g}$ by its concavity and conclude that the optimal measurement should be rank one.
L000923	Thus, in the following, we let ${\rm rank}(M_{a|x})=1~\forall a,x$.
L000924	
L000925	Combining with the case that $\rho^{\rm AB}\to \psi^{AB}$ is pure,
L000926	we have the conditional states $\{\psi_{a|x}\}_{a,x}$ on Bob's side are pure.
L000927	In such case, we can further compute the optimal violation degree of pure bipartite input $\psi^{AB}$ as follows:
L000928	\begin{equation}
L000929	\begin{aligned}
L000930	    \mathcal{S}_{g}(\psi^{AB})
L000931	    &= \max \left\{  \max_{\{p(a|x),\psi_{a|x}\}} \sum_{a} p(a|x) g(\psi_{a|x}) - \min_{\{p(a|x),\psi_{a|x}\}} \sum_{a}p(a|x)F_{g}(\psi_{a|x})  ,0 \right\} \\
L000932	    &=   \max_{\{p(a|x),\psi_{a|x}\}} \sum_{a} p(a|x) F_g(\psi_{a|x}) - \min_{\{p(a|x),\psi_{a|x}\}} \sum_{a}p(a|x)g(\psi_{a|x})  \\
L000933	    &\le F_{g}\left[\sum_{a} p(a|x^{\star}) \psi_{a|x^{\star}}\right] - g\left[\sum_{a}p(a|x_{\star})\psi_{a|x_{\star}}\right]\\
L000934	    &= F_{g} (\rho^{\rm B}) - g(\rho^{\rm B}),
L000935	\end{aligned}
L000936	\end{equation}
L000937	where the second line is given by $F(\psi)=g(\psi)$ such that it is non-negative; the third line is taken by optimized when $x=x^{\star}$ ($x=x_{\star}$) is maximum (minimum) function, respectively, and the equation holds if the concave (convex) roof is achievable.
L000938	According to Ref.~\cite{Horodecki2009rmp}, a function of $\rho^{\rm B}$ satisfies strong monotonicity under local operation an classical communication (LOCC) if and only if (i) it is concave, and (ii) it is symmetry (under permutations of its arguments) and expandable.
L000939	For (i), notice that given $g$ is convex and $F_g$ is concave, therefore
L000940	\begin{equation}
L000941	\begin{aligned}
L000942	    [F_{g}-g][t\rho_{1}+(1-t)\rho_{2}]
L000943	    &= F_{g}[t\rho_{1}+(1-t)\rho_{2}] - g[t\rho_{1}+(1-t)\rho_{2}] \\
L000944	    &\geq tF_{g}(\rho_{1})+(1-t)F_{g}(\rho_{2}) - tg(\rho_{1}) - (1-t)g(\rho_{2}) \\
L000945	    &= t[F_{g}-g](\rho_{1})+(1-t)[F_{g}-g](\rho_{2}),
L000946	\end{aligned}
L000947	\end{equation}
L000948	which indicates $[F_{g}-g]$ is concave.
L000949	For (ii), as we require the function $g$ to be symmetric and expandable, its concave roof and the derivative function $[F_{g}-g]$ are also symmetric and expandable.
L000950	Thus, we can conclude that $\mathcal{S}_{g}$ is monotonic under LOCC for an given convex, symmetric, and expandable $g$.
L000951	
L000952	In addition, any function that is convex and symmetric is Schur-convex, while any function that is concave and symmetric is Schur-concave~\cite{RobertsVarberg1973,Bhatia1997,Nielsen1999}.
L000953	In our case, the quantity $[F_g-g]$ is concave and symmetric, hence it is Schur-concave in the spectrum of the relevant state (e.g., in the eigenvalues of $\rho_B$).
L000954	Since the maximally mixed spectrum $u=(1/d,\ldots,1/d)$ of a $d$-dimension system is majorized by every other spectrum ($u\prec p$), Schur-concavity implies $[F_g-g](u)\ge [F_g-g](p)$ for all $p$.
L000955	Therefore the violation attains its maximum when the input marginal is maximally mixed.
L000956	For pure bipartite inputs, $\rho_B$ is maximally mixed if and only if the global state is maximally entangled; consequently the algebraic maximum of the violation is achieved only for maximally entangled inputs, i.e.,
L000957	\begin{equation}
L000958	    \mathcal{S}_g^{\rm max} = [F_g - g](\frac{\id}{d}),
L000959	\end{equation}
L000960	with system dimension $d$.
